        "title": "About Aviation Safety",
        "subject": "Aviation Safety",
        "summary": "Aviation safety concerns the regulation, operation and investigation of civil aircraft activities intended to prevent accidents and incidents. In the Cape Town context, reports of aircraft operating unusually low over a stadium raise questions about compliance with South African flight rules and whether an occurrence warrants regulatory or safety-investigation action.",
        "facts": [
          {
            "label": "International framework",
            "value": "Aircraft accident and incident investigations are governed internationally by ICAO Annex 13, whose stated purpose is prevention and improvement of aviation safety—not the assignment of blame or liability.",
            "source_ids": [
              "o1",
              "o2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "South African regulator",
            "value": "The South African Civil Aviation Authority regulates civil-aviation safety and security under the Civil Aviation Act, 2009, including oversight of operators, airworthiness, airports, airspace and flight procedures.",
            "source_ids": [
              "o3",
              "o4"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Investigation and reporting",
            "value": "South Africa's Accident and Incident Investigations Division says safety-affecting occurrences must be reported; its investigations are intended to prevent recurrence and are not mandated to establish blame or liability.",
            "source_ids": [
              "o5"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Minimum-height rules",
            "value": "Except for takeoff, landing or prior written approval, South African rules generally prohibit aircraft from flying below 1,000 feet above the highest obstacle over congested areas or open-air assemblies, and prohibit circling or repeated overflights of such assemblies below 3,000 feet above the surface.",
            "source_ids": [
              "o6",
              "o7"
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "Which state leads an investigation",
            "value": "Under the Chicago Convention and ICAO procedures, the state where an accident occurs normally institutes the inquiry; the states of registry, operator, design and manufacture may participate under defined rights and responsibilities.",
